   ====================================================
   CSS FONT SHORTHAND — COMPLETE NOTES
   ====================================================

   1. Definition:
      - The font shorthand property in CSS allows you to 
        set multiple font-related properties in a single line.
      - It can include:
           font-style, font-variant, font-weight, font-stretch,
           font-size, line-height, and font-family.
      - font-size and font-family are required; all others 
        are optional but must precede font-size if used.

   2. Required properties:
      - font-size
      - font-family
      (All other properties are optional but must precede font-size)

   3. Complete order of properties (CSS standard):
      [font-style] || [font-variant] || [font-weight] || [font-stretch] font-size [/line-height] font-family
      - '||' means the properties are optional and can appear in any order before font-size.
      - font-family must come last.
      - font-size is required.
      - line-height is optional and comes after font-size, separated by '/'

   4. Line-height:
      - Optional, comes immediately after font-size separated by "/"
      Example: 16px/1.5

   5. Valid examples:
      font: 16px Arial;
      font: italic 700 18px/1.4 "Poppins", sans-serif;
      font: normal small-caps bold 20px/1.2 "Inter", sans-serif;

   6. Invalid examples:
      font: Arial 16px;       /* wrong order */
      font: bold Arial;       /* missing size */
      font: 16px;             /* missing family */

   7. Important notes:
      - Using font shorthand resets unspecified properties 
        (style, variant, weight, stretch, line-height) to defaults.
      - Always maintain correct order to avoid errors.
      - font-size and font-family are always required.

